everyone will remember stan lee as the  lovable grandpa of the marvel universe  are you tony's stank all those wonderful  cameos aside  stan lee was a cornerstone of the comics  industry dating all the way back to  1939.  for eight decades lee compiled a  remarkable list of achievements and  changed comics forever but there's still  plenty that many marvel fans don't know  about his life and legacy  let's take some time to remember and  appreciate stan lee for introducing us  to earth's mightiest heroes  stan lee was born stanley martin lieber  in 1922 which was perfect timing to  enter the workforce during the great  depression  he was always interested in writing and  when he turned 18 he got a job with  timely comics  the company that would later become  marvel it just happened to be owned by  martin goodman who was married to lee's  cousin  how much this family connection helped  lee get the job remains disputed  at the time lee assumed his career in  comics would be short-lived  and worried that it might hurt his  ambition to become a respected novelist  as a result he adopted stan lee as a pen  name and spent years introducing himself  to people simply as a writer  without telling them what he wrote lee  had been working at marvel for less than  a year when joe simon and jack kirby  discovered that martin goodman had been  stiffing them out of royalties on their  popular creation captain america  outraged kirby and simon quit timely for  national comics the company that would  later become dc  goodman immediately appointed the 19  year old stan lee to replace simon as  editor-in-chief  at the time he had mostly written short  filler comics and brief text stories  that were only included so the comics  could get a cheaper rate on postage  according to lee the appointment was  supposed to be temporary but goodman  became distracted with other branches of  his publishing company  and forgot to replace him as it turned  out lee remained in the post for the  next  three decades although like most comics  creators of his generation he did take a  few years out to serve in the army  during world war  ii it was there that he had his greatest  fame as an artist  designing posters warning soldiers  against sexually transmitted diseases  with the catchy caption vd not me  after the war superheroes suffered a  shockingly rapid decline in popularity  and over a timely all superhero comics  were cancelled  lee desperate to stay in business had  little room for creativity  despite efforts to copy other company's  success with westerns and monster comics  the company repeatedly risked bankruptcy  during the 1950s to the point that jack  kirby said he once came in to find lee  sobbing while movers were taking  furniture out of the office  goodman eventually made lee fire all the  other employees including many of his  closest friends  but kept lee himself around when lee was  39 he told his wife joan that he wanted  to quit  but dc had recently reinvigorated  superhero comics with their new team  book the justice league of america  in an attempt to replicate those sales  lee and jack kirby co-created the  fantastic four  a huge hit that saved the company and  changed comics history  with steve ditko stan had co-created  spider-man and doctor strange  but by the mid-60s their relationship  had deteriorated to the point of being  almost non-existent  ditko had always been a little intense  but as his tenure on amazing spider-man  continued  he attempted to inject the stories with  references to ein rand's objectivist  philosophy  in which he was an ardent believer that  put him at odds with lee  and eventually they stopped speaking to  each other even as they continued  working on the comic  the final straw came when the two  creators disagreed over the true  identity of the green goblin  lee wanted it to be norman osborne the  father of spider-man's best friend  ditko allegedly wanted the goblin to be  an unrelated criminal  lee won the argument ditko exited the  book and was replaced by john romita  senior who stayed with marvel many years  despite their disputes lee and ditko  were two forces to be reckoned with  and both left us in the same year ditko  passed away on june 29th of a heart  attack at the age of 90  while lee followed a few months later on  november 12th at the age of 95.  on the page stan lee and jack kirby may  have been the team responsible for  building the majority of the marvel  universe and telling some of the best  comic book stories of all time  but behind the scenes kirby's  frustration with his collaborator was  growing throughout the 60s  to kirby stan was the consummate  corporate creator serving as the  editor-in-chief in the public face of  the company that kirby saw as exploiting  his talent  when he started at dc kirby wasted no  time in making his feelings known by  turning his collaborator  turned nemesis into a comics character  in the pages of mr miracle number six  kirby introduced funky flashman telling  readers i know him  you know him but does anybody need him  accompanied by a sniveling minion named  house roy thought to be based on stan's  assistant roy thomas  flashman was a con man out to exploit  others talent  and was first seen taking an allowance  that was automatically dispensed from a  statue  literally pulling the money out of  someone else's head despite the bad  blood kirby and lee were able to make  peace in 1993 shortly before kirby's  passing  i don't think there's any artist in the  field today who doesn't owe jack  something  in the 1960s marvel was just a small  division of a company called magazine  management and they weren't actually the  most profitable part of the company  that honor went to a line of men's  magazines with extremely creative names  like stag  mail and complete man sensing an  opportunity to capitalize on that  success  lee decided to combine magazine  management's two most prominent  departments  the result was the adventures of  pussycat which made its debut in 1965 as  what lee called  a nudie cutie comic oh  man while lee's gamble on the adventures  of pussycat didn't really pay off the  scandalous story included work from lee  wallywood and al hartley who wound up  leaving pussycat and marvel after  finding religion  he spent his remaining career drawing  christian-themed archie comics  it's easy to argue that stan's most  important role wasn't as a comic book  writer or  editor but as an ambassador for the  medium long before he was a notable  cameo in the world's most popular movies  he served his ambassador role with a set  of videos designed to give viewers a  look behind the scenes of the comic book  industry  released on vhs in 1991 the comic book  greats was a 12-part documentary series  hosted by lee where he interviewed  creators about their process  not only did it feature legends like  batman co-creator bob kane  it also put the spotlight on some of the  hottest creators of the era  among the most interesting segments are  stan's interviews with jim lee  todd mcfarlane rob liefeld and wills  portacio  four of the artists whose incredible  success in marvel would lead them to  found image comics  mcfarland and lifefeld's interviews even  managed to feature obscure first  appearances of characters that would  later make their way into the comics  challenged by stan to create a whole new  hero or villain on a tight time limit  lyfeld drew diehard a future member of  his super team youngblood  and quickly had a cheerful stan asking  questions about what he called our new  character  stan always had a knack for following  trends so it's no surprise that in 1998  he co-founded a company called stan lee  media in order to produce  webisodes for an internet audience  including an animated comic about the  backstreet boys  i hate when that happens it also  shouldn't be surprising that in 2000  when the dot-com bubble burst  slm went defunct and filed for  bankruptcy what happened after that  however nobody saw coming  the short version is that slm claimed  that lee assigned all of the rights to  his characters to the company and  they've been suing marvel  disney and lee himself for billions of  dollars since 2007. do what i ask  for earth and your friends are doomed  it probably goes without saying but the  lawsuits and appeals have consistently  been called frivolous and dismissed in  court  lee himself did his best to extract  himself from both the company and its  ongoing legal mess  unfortunately the one thing that isn't  in dispute is that they own the name  stan lee media linking lee's name to  their actions despite his best efforts  to shut them down  ah forget it the ups and downs of stan  lee's career aside he co-created and  helped introduce some of our favorite  characters to the world  with the help of late great legends like  jack kirby and steve ditko stan lee made  the fantastic four  spider-man the x-men iron man thor black  panther the hulk  and many other iconic characters  household names as the marvel cinematic  universe is starting to eclipse its  printed counterparts in terms of pop  culture influence and recognition  we should remember stan lee for being  the wonderful grandfather of superhero  dumb that he was  whether it was being the ambassador to  the comic book industry or giving peter  parker amazing advice on screen  i guess one person can make a difference  enough said
 

